  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PROTECTION OF PRIORITY HABITAT OF THE CH</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">PROFAUNA aims to increase the local abundance of three focal species by 15 percent in the next ten years: Long-billed Curlew, Mountain Plover, and Sprague&apos;s Pipit. These species are extremely faithful to their wintering grounds and rely heavily on colonies of the Mexican prairie dog, which provide them with healthy grasslands. Mexican prairie dogs have been eradicated in more than 45 percent of their original range for Tokio Prairie Land Priority Region due to habitat loss and degradation. In Janos Chihuahua the black-tailed prairie dog was reduced by 90 percent in its range, which also affects the habitat of focal species. The primary threat to mitigate is habitat destruction due to livestock overgrazing leading to soil erosion, a decrease in vegetation cover and biodiversity loss. This project focuses on three specific objectives: 1) Protection of critical habitat for grassland birds with the implementation of land or land lease actions, protection of critical habitat with the incorporation of private properties into the scheme of wildlife conservation units, diagnosis of strategic areas for conservation of grasslands; 2) Management and implementation of good agricultural and livestock practices in the conservation of birds in two Areas of Importance for the Conservation of Birds and Biodiversity, including the evaluation and monitoring of biological lines to measure the changes that occur in habitats, especially the vegetation cover and characteristics of the bird populations; and 3) Increased community awareness, where prairie dog and grassland bird colonies are distributed, on issues of protection and conservation of the different species.</narrative>
  </description>
